1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,000
For over six years, it was one of the best kept secrets in motorcycling.

2
00:00:07,001 --> 00:00:11,000
A Harley that was unlike anything Harley had ever built.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:22,000
When it was finally unveiled in the summer of 2001, the new bike came with a long list of firsts.

4
00:00:22,001 --> 00:00:27,000
Harley's first liquid-cooled engine, its first exposed steel and aluminum body,

5
00:00:27,001 --> 00:00:34,000
aluminum body, the first Harley to do 115 horsepower straight out of the box.

6
00:00:34,500 --> 00:00:39,500
They called it the V-Rod.

7
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:55,200
It all started with the idea of doing something that had never been done before.

8
00:00:55,201 --> 00:00:59,620
For almost a hundred years, Harley Davidson had put out some of the most classic motorcycles

9
00:00:59,621 --> 00:01:03,420
ever built.

10
00:01:03,421 --> 00:01:10,300
In many ways, they became a symbol of the American spirit itself.

11
00:01:10,301 --> 00:01:18,960
By the 1990s, Harleys were so popular that you needed to be on a waiting list just to

12
00:01:18,961 --> 00:01:20,460
buy one.

13
00:01:20,461 --> 00:01:25,380
Despite this success, Harley was losing ground to a new generation of riders, more interested

14
00:01:25,381 --> 00:01:29,080
in speed than in style.

15
00:01:29,081 --> 00:01:33,940
At a meeting of Harley's product development committee, the idea began to take shape.

16
00:01:33,941 --> 00:01:37,440
Was it possible to combine the best of both worlds?

17
00:01:37,441 --> 00:01:41,800
The speed and power of a performance bike wrapped up in a style that was unmistakably

18
00:01:41,801 --> 00:01:44,060
Harley.

19
00:01:44,061 --> 00:01:48,060
The top secret project was codenamed P4.

20
00:01:48,061 --> 00:01:55,900
We were really interested in reaching out to an even broader base of customers.

21
00:01:55,901 --> 00:01:59,000
Everybody had their own concept in what this thing was going to be, but we knew what the

22
00:01:59,001 --> 00:02:01,180
essence of this motorcycle was going to be.

23
00:02:01,181 --> 00:02:06,020
Bare bones, powerful, custom.

24
00:02:06,021 --> 00:02:10,820
To get the performance we wanted and the acceleration we wanted and all the things we wanted out

25
00:02:10,821 --> 00:02:14,380
of this motorcycle, we realized we had to do something new.

26
00:02:14,381 --> 00:02:18,980
To find that something new, they turned to Harley's Superbike Racing Team, which had

27
00:02:18,981 --> 00:02:25,860
spent years building and perfecting its own radical racing engine, the VR1000.

28
00:02:25,861 --> 00:02:30,420
Until now, every stock engine Harley had ever put on a bike had been air-cooled.

29
00:02:30,421 --> 00:02:35,620
The racing team's VR1000 was liquid-cooled, which meant it could rev higher and go faster,

30
00:02:35,621 --> 00:02:38,140
and that was just the beginning.

31
00:02:38,141 --> 00:02:39,700
It had dual overhead cams.

32
00:02:39,701 --> 00:02:41,320
It had four valves per cylinder.

33
00:02:41,321 --> 00:02:44,720
but was a dramatic departure from our heritage.

34
00:02:45,820 --> 00:02:48,020
And all of a sudden we're getting into this new territory

35
00:02:48,021 --> 00:02:49,620
and the challenge we're facing is,

36
00:02:49,621 --> 00:02:53,220
how do we keep the allegiance of our tried and true customer?

37
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:58,220
Until you've been on a Harley Davidson,

38
00:02:59,220 --> 00:03:04,220
you haven't been on a motor cycle.

39
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:07,620
Harley owners, for all their colorful lifestyles,

40
00:03:07,621 --> 00:03:09,920
are quite possibly the most loyal customers

41
00:03:09,921 --> 00:03:12,380
any product anywhere.

42
00:03:12,381 --> 00:03:15,740
The biggest risk in this project from the beginning

43
00:03:15,741 --> 00:03:20,300
was how could we make a bike that would perform differently

44
00:03:20,301 --> 00:03:23,180
and be a very different type of motorcycle,

45
00:03:23,181 --> 00:03:25,700
but one that still says, I'm a Harley.

46
00:03:28,360 --> 00:03:29,780
When you saw it going down the street,

47
00:03:29,781 --> 00:03:31,280
it had to scream Harley Davidson.

48
00:03:31,281 --> 00:03:33,480
When you heard it, it had to say Harley Davidson.

49
00:03:37,280 --> 00:03:39,240
To pull this off, the P4 project

50
00:03:39,241 --> 00:03:41,640
was turned over to Harley's styling department,

51
00:03:41,641 --> 00:03:44,080
led by the legendary Willie G. Davidson.

52
00:03:45,340 --> 00:03:48,160
In order to keep this bike a Harley,

53
00:03:48,161 --> 00:03:51,220
it had to have the Willie G touch.

54
00:03:51,221 --> 00:03:55,940
And that's something magic that we have,

55
00:03:55,941 --> 00:04:00,300
that nobody else has, that's a cornerstone of this company.

56
00:04:00,301 --> 00:04:02,700
We don't often have the opportunity

57
00:04:02,701 --> 00:04:06,700
of concepting an entire motorcycle,

58
00:04:06,701 --> 00:04:09,660
But this time we can really get into the details.

59
00:04:10,440 --> 00:04:12,720
Grandson of one of the founders, Willie G.

60
00:04:12,721 --> 00:04:16,520
left Milwaukee in the late 1950s to go to design school in California.

61
00:04:17,280 --> 00:04:22,360
In 1963, the prodigal son returned to become Harley's first VP of styling,

62
00:04:22,740 --> 00:04:26,940
where he proceeded to light a fire under the idea of what a Harley was supposed to look like.

63
00:04:26,941 --> 00:04:30,640
Blue and moan and green.

64
00:04:30,641 --> 00:04:36,880
In the 60s, many Harley owners were turning their bikes into customized works of art.

65
00:04:38,880 --> 00:04:42,460
Instead of being offended that the company's bikes were being chopped apart,

66
00:04:42,980 --> 00:04:44,160
Willie G was inspired.

67
00:04:45,120 --> 00:04:49,120
He went back to Milwaukee to create the Superglide, the Lowrider,

68
00:04:49,121 --> 00:04:53,160
and a whole new style of bikes that came to be called Factory Customs.

69
00:04:53,640 --> 00:04:59,360
It was a move that helped launch Harley Davidson from the brink of bankruptcy to unprecedented success.

70
00:05:00,640 --> 00:05:07,440
And for the next quarter century, he personally oversaw the design of every new model Harley turned out.

71
00:05:08,240 --> 00:05:17,280
That gives it a forward motion, and it really borrows from dragsters visually, which I think are very exciting.

72
00:05:18,560 --> 00:05:20,434
In the summer of 1995, Willie G.

73
00:05:20,435 --> 00:05:24,300
and his team of stylists took on Harley's greatest challenge ever,

74
00:05:25,180 --> 00:05:27,860
designing a brand new motorcycle from the ground up.

75
00:05:27,861 --> 00:05:35,860
This long front end is going to give the bike a side profile that will be very recognizable.

76
00:05:35,861 --> 00:05:38,860
If we don't get the frame right, the body won't follow.

77
00:05:38,861 --> 00:05:40,860
We have to get the frame right first.

78
00:05:40,861 --> 00:05:46,860
But we have to have some sense of where the body wants to be so that we can do the frame.

79
00:05:46,861 --> 00:05:52,860
I wanted this vehicle to have that type of presence where you'd walk up to it and say,

80
00:05:52,861 --> 00:05:58,800
say that's a new Harley Davidson wow

81
00:06:00,920 --> 00:06:06,000
since speed was a key design element the team found inspiration in another kind

82
00:06:06,001 --> 00:06:09,800
of racing bike dragsters

83
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:16,220
Louie Nets had been Willie's second-in-command since the late 70s drag

84
00:06:16,221 --> 00:06:21,340
racing motorcycles have a certain feel to them that's very low and long

85
00:06:21,341 --> 00:06:28,340
aggressive, minimal. So then that starts to create a sense of direction.

86
00:06:28,341 --> 00:06:33,340
When you consider the amount of real estate looking at the side profile and the impact...

87
00:06:33,341 --> 00:06:40,340
Kirk Rasmussen was brought on board to transform the ideas and sketches into three-dimensional computer-aided designs.

88
00:06:40,341 --> 00:06:45,340
So we'll be seeing this in 3D and there's some interesting things going on from a side view.

89
00:06:45,341 --> 00:06:51,540
The youngest member of the team, Frank Savage, had joined the styling department just a few months earlier.

90
00:06:52,240 --> 00:06:56,720
He would cut his teeth on a project that every young designer in America could only wish for.

91
00:06:56,900 --> 00:06:58,640
This simulates more of the rank of the vehicle.

92
00:06:58,641 --> 00:07:00,640
It looks fast. It's great.

93
00:07:00,760 --> 00:07:01,460
It does.

94
00:07:01,461 --> 00:07:02,161
It's good.

95
00:07:03,560 --> 00:07:08,920
That was just so exciting that I can be at the ground level of this thing and watch it rise.

96
00:07:09,080 --> 00:07:10,220
Just a dream come true.

97
00:07:10,500 --> 00:07:14,820
I mean, you could try a lifetime to get into a project like this and it wouldn't happen.

98
00:07:15,340 --> 00:07:25,520
Early on, they came up with their own nickname for the P4, Digger.

99
00:07:25,521 --> 00:07:31,200
We like the name Digger. It just has the right sound. I think it fits the bike.

100
00:07:32,960 --> 00:07:37,240
The process of designing a motorcycle is a balance of art and science.

101
00:07:38,320 --> 00:07:42,440
At Harley's newly opened product development center, another team was being assembled,

102
00:07:42,900 --> 00:07:44,260
a select group of engineers.

103
00:07:45,340 --> 00:07:50,340
It would be their job to turn styling's designs into a working motorcycle.

104
00:07:50,341 --> 00:07:58,340
The job of keeping the project on time and on budget was given to Harley's chief engineer of motorcycles, Pat Keller.

105
00:07:58,341 --> 00:08:04,340
I was excited because this was a chance to do something really new and meaningful for Harley-Davidson.

106
00:08:04,341 --> 00:08:09,340
And I was really looking forward to a chance to make some history with this project.

107
00:08:09,341 --> 00:08:13,580
Jeff Romanesco, who had spent the last six years engineering Harley's touring bikes,

108
00:08:13,820 --> 00:08:16,720
was put in charge of the team that would build the P4's chassis.

109
00:08:17,320 --> 00:08:21,960
To be able to take a clean sheet of paper and develop a whole new motorcycle is just incredible.

110
00:08:23,480 --> 00:08:28,080
Working with him was a small group of engineers who were sworn to keep the project secret,

111
00:08:28,560 --> 00:08:29,660
even from their families.

112
00:08:30,620 --> 00:08:34,780
At this point, no one could begin to fathom the energy or the man hours

113
00:08:34,781 --> 00:08:37,680
it would take over the next six years to create this new Harley.

114
00:08:37,681 --> 00:08:44,220
putting a vr 1000 race engine into a street chassis meant that it was going to have to

115
00:08:44,221 --> 00:08:53,340
be quite a bit different from a traditional Harley one of the first challenges was understanding what

116
00:08:53,341 --> 00:08:58,560
has to be involved to make a Harley Davidson a Harley Davidson you know how far can we take

117
00:08:58,561 --> 00:09:05,040
this clean sheet approach their first step was to put a vr 1000 racing engine into the frame of a

118
00:09:05,041 --> 00:09:06,040
traditional Harley.

119
00:09:06,041 --> 00:09:12,600
So the styling department came and took a look at it and then after about five minutes,

120
00:09:12,601 --> 00:09:16,720
and we had spent weeks building it, after about five minutes said, this looks like a

121
00:09:16,721 --> 00:09:20,420
soft tail with a liquid cooled engine in it, that's not what we want, start over.

122
00:09:20,421 --> 00:09:25,500
It really told us very strongly what not to do.

123
00:09:25,501 --> 00:09:30,980
The traditional look of a Harley was not intended for that engine.

124
00:09:30,981 --> 00:09:34,500
That's kind of where we started to talk about, well, you know, we need to do something completely

125
00:09:34,501 --> 00:09:39,300
different appearances aside there was also a practical problem the traditional

126
00:09:39,301 --> 00:09:43,320
Harley frame wasn't sturdy enough to handle the considerably more powerful

127
00:09:43,321 --> 00:09:47,400
engine we knew the bike was going to be fast and powerful and we knew the frame

128
00:09:47,401 --> 00:09:52,620
had to be very strong and very stable for that to work Jeff Romanesco assigned

129
00:09:52,621 --> 00:09:56,680
the job of designing the new frame to second generation Harley engineer Rob

130
00:09:56,681 --> 00:10:01,740
Dennert who had started with the company as a summer intern 12 years earlier this

131
00:10:01,741 --> 00:10:04,440
motorcycle would be capable of speeds much higher than anything we were

132
00:10:04,441 --> 00:10:05,511
building today.

133
00:10:05,512 --> 00:10:10,440
So we needed the structure that would allow handling at those speeds.

134
00:10:10,441 --> 00:10:12,840
It also needed to look like a Harley.

135
00:10:12,841 --> 00:10:20,500
We wanted the frame to be simple, direct. We wanted the frame to enhance the engine.

136
00:10:20,501 --> 00:10:23,980
Willie G once said to me, I want something that I could hang up on the wall in and of

137
00:10:23,981 --> 00:10:29,840
itself and let it be a piece of art. That was a real challenge for us because frames

138
00:10:29,841 --> 00:10:33,620
are something that you attach things to, that provide structure, that connect the front

139
00:10:33,621 --> 00:10:34,766
wheel to the rear wheel.

140
00:10:34,767 --> 00:10:38,060
We had to do all those things and make it look good at the same time.

141
00:10:38,860 --> 00:10:44,660
After weeks of CAD drawings, Dennert hit upon a solution, an elegantly curved double rail.

142
00:10:45,720 --> 00:10:50,060
In a traditional Harley, a single frame rail is hidden beneath the gas tank.

143
00:10:50,540 --> 00:10:55,240
To accommodate the additional horsepower, the P4 would have two rails that would run along

144
00:10:55,241 --> 00:11:00,600
the outside of the bike. As a bonus, Willie G. and his styling team loved the idea.

145
00:11:01,500 --> 00:11:07,480
We decided we would just take advantage of that and try to make the frame a part of the styling.

146
00:11:08,100 --> 00:11:10,380
The only question was, could it be built?

147
00:11:11,400 --> 00:11:16,940
There really are no straight or curved sections that are defined by typical lines and arcs.

148
00:11:17,180 --> 00:11:19,220
For engineers, we like nice straight sections.

149
00:11:19,580 --> 00:11:22,240
Adding a curve or a shape to it complicates the mix.

150
00:11:22,440 --> 00:11:27,560
In order to get this kind of shape, we're going to need a tubing process that we don't use on our curved frames.

151
00:11:27,561 --> 00:11:31,400
The problem was that steel tubes can only be bent so far.

152
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:37,100
Complex curves require welding separate pieces together, which makes for a weaker frame.

153
00:11:37,760 --> 00:11:40,740
We were outside the tube bending manual at that point in time.

154
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:46,180
While the engineers struggled to find a way to avoid bending the frame tubes to their breaking point,

155
00:11:46,540 --> 00:11:48,880
they also began to ask a more basic question.

156
00:11:49,380 --> 00:11:52,660
How well would their emerging design work on a real live bike?

157
00:11:53,660 --> 00:11:56,280
After the styling concept kind of got gelled,

158
00:11:56,281 --> 00:11:58,220
we knew what we wanted to do with the frame.

159
00:11:58,420 --> 00:12:00,700
We said, you know, we need to take that basic concept,

160
00:12:00,980 --> 00:12:04,320
put it into steel, and actually run the motorcycle.

161
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:12,200
A crude version of the frame was welded together in Harley's machine shop

162
00:12:12,201 --> 00:12:14,680
and married up with a VR1000 engine

163
00:12:14,681 --> 00:12:17,020
to become the new bike's first working prototype.

164
00:12:20,620 --> 00:12:22,700
Do you think we really need two fasteners there?

165
00:12:23,780 --> 00:12:24,480
I bet one.

166
00:12:24,500 --> 00:12:25,960
Are you worried about it pivoting on there?

167
00:12:25,961 --> 00:12:27,560
I suppose you had the tab anyway.

168
00:12:29,700 --> 00:12:32,600
To get the dragster look the styling was asking for,

169
00:12:32,900 --> 00:12:35,500
they wanted to see how far they could push out the front end.

170
00:12:37,160 --> 00:12:40,060
On a motorcycle, the more raked out the front end is,

171
00:12:40,220 --> 00:12:41,560
the harder it is to handle.

172
00:12:42,000 --> 00:12:43,500
To get the best of both worlds,

173
00:12:43,800 --> 00:12:47,080
they offset the angle between the steering head and the front fork.

174
00:12:48,420 --> 00:12:51,220
That gave us the long, low, raked out front end look

175
00:12:51,221 --> 00:12:53,160
of the dragster custom motorcycle

176
00:12:53,161 --> 00:12:57,960
and still gave us the handling characteristics we need to be able to live with it every day.

177
00:12:58,900 --> 00:13:01,920
The prototype brought them face-to-face with other issues.

178
00:13:02,580 --> 00:13:06,780
To make room for the engine's two large throttle valves, the fuel tank had to move.

179
00:13:07,860 --> 00:13:13,720
The challenge for engineering was, if the fuel tank couldn't go where it usually went, then where do you put it?

180
00:13:13,721 --> 00:13:18,780
By doing some competitive analysis on other bikes and brainstorming sessions,

181
00:13:18,920 --> 00:13:20,720
we realized that we could put the fuel under the seat.

182
00:13:20,721 --> 00:13:25,940
This created a new problem. With a big engine and a low frame, there wasn't much

183
00:13:25,941 --> 00:13:27,417
room left for a gas tank.

184
00:13:27,418 --> 00:13:31,080
We had essentially a Drakester fuel tank that we had purchased

185
00:13:31,081 --> 00:13:33,868
out of a catalog. It held one gallon of fuel.

186
00:13:33,869 --> 00:13:36,780
Another issue was a piece of gear no Harley had

187
00:13:36,781 --> 00:13:39,768
ever had to contend with before, a radiator.

188
00:13:39,769 --> 00:13:42,960
We used a VR1000 radiator, packaged that on the

189
00:13:42,961 --> 00:13:46,737
front in any way we could to get some air to it.

190
00:13:46,738 --> 00:13:50,120
It's going to be interesting to see what we

191
00:13:50,121 --> 00:13:51,120
actually end up with.

192
00:13:51,121 --> 00:13:55,120
This should be full extension, so I think we'll be okay.

193
00:13:55,121 --> 00:14:00,900
With cobbled together exhaust pipes and jerry-rigged wiring, it wasn't pretty, but the first incarnation

194
00:14:00,901 --> 00:14:10,000
of Harley's groundbreaking motorcycle was ready for its maiden voyage.

195
00:14:10,001 --> 00:14:14,780
It happened one quiet weekday afternoon way out in the country at Jeff Romanesco's house.

196
00:14:14,781 --> 00:14:18,540
There weren't very many homes and I knew nobody would be home at the time.

197
00:14:18,541 --> 00:14:25,540
And we cooked out some brats and rode a motorcycle in the afternoon.

198
00:14:36,880 --> 00:14:41,740
Wasn't a refined machine, but you could tell the potential.

199
00:14:41,741 --> 00:14:44,760
The prototype had one undeniable quality.

200
00:14:44,761 --> 00:14:46,760
It was fast.

201
00:14:46,761 --> 00:14:50,200
It had just an incredible rush.

202
00:14:50,500 --> 00:14:52,240
Almost had to be paying attention to make sure you hang on

203
00:14:52,241 --> 00:14:54,560
so you didn't get blown off the back of the things.

204
00:14:56,700 --> 00:14:58,040
It was a red car.

205
00:15:00,540 --> 00:15:01,580
Oh, almost.

206
00:15:03,080 --> 00:15:04,780
It was pretty euphoric.

207
00:15:05,080 --> 00:15:07,740
We knew that this was going to be something special.

208
00:15:16,760 --> 00:15:27,090
To power its new top-secret high-performance motorcycle, codename P4, Harley-Davidson wanted to make a street version of the liquid-cooled engine used in its VR1000

209
00:15:27,091 --> 00:15:27,791
superbike.

210
00:15:31,920 --> 00:15:34,740
This would prove to be easier said than done.

211
00:15:41,400 --> 00:15:46,540
Jeff Coughlin was part of the engineering team assigned the daunting task of pulling it off.

212
00:15:46,760 --> 00:15:50,560
The race engine is nearly wide up in throttle all the time.

213
00:15:50,860 --> 00:15:53,900
You're pushing the limits so you have very light components.

214
00:15:54,420 --> 00:15:58,240
You only need it to last 400 miles to get you through that weekend.

215
00:15:58,560 --> 00:16:00,985
Race engines also aren't required to meet U.S.

216
00:16:00,986 --> 00:16:03,780
emissions and noise standards, and they're expensive.

217
00:16:04,320 --> 00:16:08,640
A race engine can cost anywhere from $40,000 to $100,000.

218
00:16:09,900 --> 00:16:14,260
To help turn the VR1000 into an engine that lasted longer than a weekend,

219
00:16:14,261 --> 00:16:18,260
ran well at street-legal speeds, and was considerably less expensive,

220
00:16:18,261 --> 00:16:22,260
Harley turned to one of the world's great engine makers, Porsche.

221
00:16:22,261 --> 00:16:27,260
We gained the additional expertise to supplement our own,

222
00:16:27,261 --> 00:16:33,260
so there are more smart people looking at how to make the best product possible.

223
00:16:35,260 --> 00:16:39,260
Porsche's engineering services facility in Weissach, Germany,

224
00:16:39,261 --> 00:16:42,260
is renowned for its performance engine designs.

225
00:16:42,261 --> 00:16:49,260
In late 1996, a team from Harley took up residence there to begin collaborating on the P4.

226
00:16:53,260 --> 00:16:59,260
Meanwhile, back in Milwaukee, Willie G and his team of stylists began working on what the new engine should look like.

227
00:16:59,261 --> 00:17:05,260
The Harley imagery has always been a very strong V-twin.

228
00:17:05,261 --> 00:17:11,260
There's never any question that from this company, the engine's going to be a styled masterpiece.

229
00:17:11,261 --> 00:17:16,620
I've always thought of them as jewelry from a visual standpoint and shape.

230
00:17:17,820 --> 00:17:19,600
I think that works quite well.

231
00:17:20,780 --> 00:17:24,520
And also, it's very obviously a V-twin.

232
00:17:26,040 --> 00:17:30,100
They began by making a clay model of what they wanted the new engine to look like.

233
00:17:30,900 --> 00:17:34,400
One purely stylistic touch that they added were air fins.

234
00:17:35,200 --> 00:17:40,000
What we're doing is playing with fins and whether we want to bring them all the way down.

235
00:17:40,001 --> 00:17:43,400
What we tried to do is look at the drag bikes, you know, those big jugs.

236
00:17:44,840 --> 00:17:47,760
One of their biggest issues was where to put the water hose.

237
00:17:48,080 --> 00:17:51,420
The difficult issue in this one was how are we going to get the water out of there?

238
00:17:51,720 --> 00:17:58,960
The cleanest technical solution was taking that water straight forward towards the radiator,

239
00:17:59,380 --> 00:18:06,680
and that would mean crossing over a cylinder at the base, and that was not a good visual.

240
00:18:07,260 --> 00:18:08,380
It really was not.

241
00:18:08,381 --> 00:18:15,080
It is coming right in front of a part of the engine that we spent time making look good.

242
00:18:15,560 --> 00:18:20,340
Their solution was to reroute the hose, taking it up and over the top of the engine.

243
00:18:20,760 --> 00:18:26,360
It just seemed a crime to not split that thing in a symmetrical way.

244
00:18:27,340 --> 00:18:32,120
And I think we maybe gave engineering a couple fits on that one.

245
00:18:32,121 --> 00:18:38,260
One of the things I like to tell the engineers when they griped about that is it's not the destination, it's the journey.

246
00:18:38,380 --> 00:18:43,160
To get a sense of what the entire bike would look like, Frank Savage built a full-scale

247
00:18:43,161 --> 00:18:48,900
model using a fiberglass frame, a couple of tires, and a whole lot of modeling clay.

248
00:18:48,901 --> 00:18:56,140
The speed at which you can create things in clay is analogous to the sketching on paper,

249
00:18:56,141 --> 00:18:57,140
the way I look at it.

250
00:18:57,141 --> 00:19:01,880
You throw down clay, you put down some lines if you don't like it, you change it that

251
00:19:01,881 --> 00:19:03,120
quick, you know.

252
00:19:03,121 --> 00:19:06,620
We can have critiques and change things in a heartbeat with clay.

253
00:19:06,621 --> 00:19:08,180
It can just happen like that, okay?

254
00:19:08,181 --> 00:19:09,058
next concept.

255
00:19:09,059 --> 00:19:14,120
The 3D model shed a glaring light on one of the bike's toughest challenges,

256
00:19:14,620 --> 00:19:17,811
the exhaust pipes, a job assigned to engineer Mike Nixdorf.

257
00:19:17,812 --> 00:19:19,380
We need to talk with the tube

258
00:19:19,381 --> 00:19:23,080
bender. I think some of the radii that we came up with there are tighter than we've

259
00:19:23,081 --> 00:19:23,908
ever done before.

260
00:19:23,909 --> 00:19:27,220
Tight radii and you've got some very minimal straight sections there

261
00:19:27,221 --> 00:19:27,921
too.

262
00:19:27,922 --> 00:19:33,280
The problem was that to meet noise regulations, it would have to be large enough to hold 12

263
00:19:33,281 --> 00:19:36,601
liters of air, the biggest exhaust ever on a Harley.

264
00:19:36,602 --> 00:19:38,900
In engineering's initial blueprints,

265
00:19:38,901 --> 00:19:44,599
it looked both mammoth and ugly. Man, those mufflers look huge.

266
00:19:44,600 --> 00:19:46,860
Styling wasn't happy with

267
00:19:46,861 --> 00:19:51,660
the fact that we had to package 12 liters for one thing, but soon when they got over that,

268
00:19:51,900 --> 00:19:56,980
they knew that with that size, it had to be the best-looking, sexiest exhaust system that we had

269
00:19:56,981 --> 00:19:57,728
ever put on a bike.

270
00:19:57,729 --> 00:20:00,640
I think Styling's going to have the last say on that no matter what we do.

271
00:20:00,641 --> 00:20:04,480
The design of the exhaust came from a sketch that Kirk did.

272
00:20:04,481 --> 00:20:09,360
Kirk was doing some swoop and really playing around with quick shapes and gestures.

273
00:20:09,361 --> 00:20:13,660
And from there, we got this sketch, like, oh, that's cool, let's try that.

274
00:20:13,661 --> 00:20:17,460
So I go to the clay and just start wheeling away at it.

275
00:20:17,461 --> 00:20:22,000
And soon we're looking at that in 3D versus the two-dimensional thing and realizing, okay,

276
00:20:22,001 --> 00:20:23,000
these things are pretty big.

277
00:20:23,001 --> 00:20:25,160
But how in the world are we going to make this transition?

278
00:20:25,161 --> 00:20:31,820
I mean, you guys have it so that the pipes are coming around, and then just we've got nothing there.

279
00:20:31,960 --> 00:20:36,560
We looked at that design, and immediately it would strike fear in the heart of any engineer.

280
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:40,200
Because looking at the clay model, it was beautiful.

281
00:20:40,940 --> 00:20:42,540
It just didn't look producible.

282
00:20:42,900 --> 00:20:47,540
As one engineer put it, it was like trying to fit 10 pounds in a 5-pound bag.

283
00:20:48,540 --> 00:20:51,180
Just remember that exhaust is such a big visual.

284
00:20:52,200 --> 00:20:52,900
Yeah, I know.

285
00:20:52,901 --> 00:20:54,620
You've got three or four feet of tubing.

286
00:20:55,160 --> 00:20:59,920
and that will really be a big identifier on this right side of the bike.

287
00:21:01,440 --> 00:21:05,400
But the biggest challenge that faced styling and engineering was just beginning.

288
00:21:06,140 --> 00:21:07,220
The radiator.

289
00:21:09,200 --> 00:21:16,780
We've never had a big piece of real estate hanging on our down tubes.

290
00:21:17,040 --> 00:21:18,760
Everybody was concerned about the radiator,

291
00:21:18,761 --> 00:21:23,580
and I think it was one of Willie's biggest concerns with the vehicle.

292
00:21:23,740 --> 00:21:24,840
We don't have radiators.

293
00:21:24,841 --> 00:21:26,000
We never had radiators.

294
00:21:26,160 --> 00:21:26,880
What are we going to do?

295
00:21:27,200 --> 00:21:32,160
We didn't want to just hang a radiator in the front of the motorcycle because everybody else does that.

296
00:21:32,780 --> 00:21:39,080
They had to come up with a way to package the radiator so that it got enough air to cool the engine and also looked great.

297
00:21:41,060 --> 00:21:47,160
The task of coming up with the initial design was turned over to engineer Joe Schaffer and CAD designer John Myers.

298
00:21:47,161 --> 00:21:54,440
It had to be styled into a Harley-Davidson, which meant that it had to be integrated into

299
00:21:54,441 --> 00:21:55,440
the vehicle.

300
00:21:55,441 --> 00:21:59,920
Being able to do that and still having a cooling system that was fully functional, had lots

301
00:21:59,921 --> 00:22:04,720
of airflow and could cool the engine, were quite a challenge to get past.

302
00:22:04,721 --> 00:22:09,160
I don't think we're going to get the airflow we need through the core.

303
00:22:09,161 --> 00:22:12,000
We're going to have to get outside further.

304
00:22:12,001 --> 00:22:14,940
Can we just make it a little bit wider?

305
00:22:16,200 --> 00:22:19,660
And, you know, the center we might as well not even have open in my mind.

306
00:22:20,000 --> 00:22:22,120
See, I'm thinking styling's going to have heartburn on that

307
00:22:22,121 --> 00:22:24,120
because I'm already an inch wider than they want it.

308
00:22:24,600 --> 00:22:29,240
Real early on, we thought maybe the best way to make it as inconspicuous as possible.

309
00:22:29,360 --> 00:22:34,180
The idea was to have it look like it's a piece of the bodywork on the motorcycle

310
00:22:34,181 --> 00:22:36,040
but like there's not a radiator there.

311
00:22:36,041 --> 00:22:37,860
Literally hide the cooling system.

312
00:22:38,100 --> 00:22:41,420
The first one we did was tiny.

313
00:22:42,000 --> 00:22:43,120
We loved that.

314
00:22:43,620 --> 00:22:48,980
A prototype of the radiator was tested in a wind tunnel to see if it got enough airflow to cool the engine.

315
00:22:49,800 --> 00:22:51,940
We discovered it flat didn't work.

316
00:22:55,820 --> 00:23:00,180
It was a validation of the fact that we just didn't have enough airflow to cool the vehicle.

317
00:23:00,580 --> 00:23:02,420
Part of the problem was the front tire.

318
00:23:03,040 --> 00:23:07,540
The front tire actually acts as an air dam, so as the motorcycle is moving,

319
00:23:07,541 --> 00:23:11,860
all the air that hits the front of the front tire kind of gets shot around to the outside.

320
00:23:12,000 --> 00:23:18,000
The engineer suggested making the radiator bigger and adding scoops to pull in air from either side of the front wheel.

321
00:23:18,001 --> 00:23:25,000
Engineers like big radiators out in the open and with lots of air flow to them.

322
00:23:25,001 --> 00:23:28,000
And they come up and say, what do you think about this?

323
00:23:28,001 --> 00:23:32,000
And it's got these scoops that are like this and real geometric.

324
00:23:32,001 --> 00:23:36,000
And we said, it looks like Gumby's casket. I don't know.

325
00:23:36,001 --> 00:23:41,000
It didn't look good. It was just these boxes of cereal out there grabbing air.

326
00:23:41,001 --> 00:23:46,280
air so what we did was we started compromising how far can we pull this back where can we trim it

327
00:23:46,281 --> 00:23:52,680
like this right follow that contour how about the bottom the bottom if you can bring it down

328
00:23:53,720 --> 00:24:01,080
and come more off like this kind of even with this right but i'm coming back saying no it's got to be

329
00:24:01,081 --> 00:24:05,480
wider it's got to be bigger i don't care if it looks the way it does just make it bigger and we

330
00:24:05,481 --> 00:24:09,880
try everything people would come in here vice presidents would come in the styling department

331
00:24:09,881 --> 00:24:11,840
and look at it and just say no.

332
00:24:12,200 --> 00:24:14,760
And then we just, okay, next version.

333
00:24:15,140 --> 00:24:17,120
There are times when we would challenge them

334
00:24:17,121 --> 00:24:19,800
that there's just no changing the laws of physics,

335
00:24:19,940 --> 00:24:22,500
and there were times that they had to remain adamant

336
00:24:22,501 --> 00:24:24,640
because there was no changing the laws of Willie.

337
00:24:36,080 --> 00:24:37,600
Two years into the project,

338
00:24:37,601 --> 00:24:44,220
the designers and engineers in Harley's top secret new motorcycle had succeeded in creating the overall look of the bike.

339
00:24:44,360 --> 00:24:46,360
The battle now was in the details.

340
00:24:47,360 --> 00:24:52,980
Engineer Mike Nixdorf took on the challenge of more than tripling the size of the prototype's one gallon gas tank.

341
00:24:55,720 --> 00:25:01,380
You can create a bike that looks awesome, but if you're only going to be able to run 50 miles on a tank,

342
00:25:02,220 --> 00:25:04,220
nobody's going to buy it.

343
00:25:04,221 --> 00:25:11,360
Are you still, on the shielding side, are you still thinking of having something on the tank itself?

344
00:25:12,160 --> 00:25:18,820
They wanted at least three and a half gallons, but the tight space left no room for a traditional steel tank of that size.

345
00:25:20,300 --> 00:25:22,320
Nick Starf suggested they try plastic.

346
00:25:23,260 --> 00:25:26,740
Plastic fuel tank and Harley don't typically go together.

347
00:25:27,320 --> 00:25:28,940
But plastic had its advantages.

348
00:25:29,520 --> 00:25:30,520
It was heat resistant.

349
00:25:31,200 --> 00:25:33,100
Being nearly seamless, it was more durable.

350
00:25:33,101 --> 00:25:37,300
and because it could be molded into every available nook and cranny,

351
00:25:37,301 --> 00:25:40,000
it could hold almost four gallons of gas.

352
00:25:41,320 --> 00:25:44,420
Meanwhile, the styling team was kicking around another idea

353
00:25:44,421 --> 00:25:46,880
that Harley had never tried before, aluminum.

354
00:25:47,820 --> 00:25:51,120
They recommended that we do the sheet metal,

355
00:25:51,520 --> 00:25:55,400
fenders and airbox cover, out of wrought aluminum.

356
00:25:55,680 --> 00:25:57,960
I'm not sure about these de-woss sections.

357
00:25:58,600 --> 00:25:59,960
I think it could be simplified.

358
00:26:00,420 --> 00:26:02,480
I really like the side elevation.

359
00:26:03,100 --> 00:26:09,640
because it's low in dimension this way and it has a lot of length.

360
00:26:10,320 --> 00:26:16,360
We wanted a finish that would enhance the performance look.

361
00:26:16,760 --> 00:26:22,560
When you think of aluminum, you think of Ferraris, you think of racing vehicles.

362
00:26:23,320 --> 00:26:29,780
It's also a material that I think, when combined with chrome, has this beauty and simplicity.

363
00:26:30,260 --> 00:26:32,440
Aluminum is a wonderful material.

364
00:26:32,441 --> 00:26:36,680
It's lightweight, it's corrosive resistant, and so forth,

365
00:26:36,681 --> 00:26:40,240
but it's very difficult to form into compound shapes.

366
00:26:40,241 --> 00:26:42,480
Frequently, when you go down some path like that,

367
00:26:42,481 --> 00:26:44,820
that is going to be difficult, the reward at the end

368
00:26:44,821 --> 00:26:46,280
is going to be that much greater.

369
00:26:46,281 --> 00:27:00,281
Part of the appeal of a Harley has always been the ability to customize it, to make

370
00:27:05,280 --> 00:27:09,240
it uniquely your own, and the V-Rod was no exception.

371
00:27:09,241 --> 00:27:13,840
Early on in its development, another group of Harley designers focused on creating parts

372
00:27:13,841 --> 00:27:18,340
and accessories that would fit the bike's highly styled look without appearing to be

373
00:27:18,341 --> 00:27:19,041
an afterthought.

374
00:27:19,560 --> 00:27:23,360
In the case of the windshield, they really want it to appear that it's just floating

375
00:27:23,361 --> 00:27:25,140
in space, that it's just there.

376
00:27:25,280 --> 00:27:30,140
If you look at it from a profile view, all you see is the slim edge, and that's it.

377
00:27:30,360 --> 00:27:31,860
You can barely tell it's on the vehicle.

378
00:27:31,861 --> 00:27:36,400
A bigger challenge was designing a saddlebag that didn't compromise the bike's lean,

379
00:27:36,560 --> 00:27:37,340
stripped-down look.

380
00:27:37,341 --> 00:27:45,500
One of the design challenges for the bags was there's no overhang for the lid to open

381
00:27:45,501 --> 00:27:46,500
properly.

382
00:27:46,501 --> 00:27:49,560
Our engineers came up with this four bar linkage hinge.

383
00:27:49,561 --> 00:27:53,880
When the saddlebag, if you're looking at it from the side, it actually lifts the lid off

384
00:27:53,881 --> 00:27:58,780
first and then pivots it forward.

385
00:27:58,781 --> 00:28:03,440
The biggest hold up now was unfortunately the most critical part of all, the heart of

386
00:28:03,441 --> 00:28:04,940
any Harley, the engine.

387
00:28:11,620 --> 00:28:13,940
When the first prototype finally arrived,

388
00:28:13,941 --> 00:28:16,520
it was put into the latest mock-up of the bike

389
00:28:16,521 --> 00:28:18,260
so it could be road tested for durability.

390
00:28:21,220 --> 00:28:23,940
The very first running prototype engines that we saw

391
00:28:23,941 --> 00:28:24,980
were somewhat disappointing.

392
00:28:27,620 --> 00:28:31,160
Designing a motorcycle is much different than designing a car.

393
00:28:31,161 --> 00:28:35,740
For example, the acceleration rates that this motorcycle has are much higher.

394
00:28:38,660 --> 00:28:44,420
Despite these setbacks, Harley remained confident in the joint team's ability to tackle the roadblocks ahead.

395
00:28:45,360 --> 00:28:49,680
So there was a strong effort from both companies to reinforce the team.

396
00:28:49,800 --> 00:28:53,400
We got a lot of Porsche's best experts assigned to the project,

397
00:28:53,620 --> 00:28:55,720
and we got a lot of involvement from Harley-Davidson,

398
00:28:55,721 --> 00:29:00,020
and together we completely redesigned the engine from that point.

399
00:29:00,021 --> 00:29:04,480
And as you take two steps forward and all of a sudden one back and sometimes two back,

400
00:29:05,080 --> 00:29:07,220
your heart starts to pitter a little bit all the time.

401
00:29:07,280 --> 00:29:10,740
How much more money is this going to take? How much more time is this going to take?

402
00:29:10,840 --> 00:29:13,840
The P4 was taking far longer than anyone had expected.

403
00:29:14,520 --> 00:29:16,740
But for every setback came a success.

404
00:29:18,560 --> 00:29:24,120
For one, the engineering team found a way to make a frame that had both the elegant curve styling wanted

405
00:29:24,121 --> 00:29:26,620
and the durability that the new engine demanded.

406
00:29:26,621 --> 00:29:32,800
We've eliminated the weld marks and the tool marks and things like that because it is now

407
00:29:32,801 --> 00:29:33,800
one piece.

408
00:29:33,801 --> 00:29:35,960
We're actually making some pretty good progress.

409
00:29:35,961 --> 00:29:41,040
I had been doing some research around different ways to fabricate materials and I came across

410
00:29:41,041 --> 00:29:43,220
an article around hydroforming.

411
00:29:43,221 --> 00:29:47,100
Hydroforming is a process that uses water pressure to bend and shape sections of pipe

412
00:29:47,101 --> 00:29:48,100
from the inside.

413
00:29:48,101 --> 00:29:53,780
What it does is it literally expands the material like a balloon to form out and fill out the

414
00:29:53,781 --> 00:29:55,740
cavity to represent the shape.

415
00:29:55,741 --> 00:30:01,220
It allowed them to reduce the number of welds from 17 to 7.

416
00:30:01,221 --> 00:30:07,140
And it really gave styling that jewel that they could hang on the wall.

417
00:30:07,141 --> 00:30:12,500
An equally ingenious solution was finally found for making a big exhaust look small.

418
00:30:12,501 --> 00:30:18,180
With two mufflers in the stylized shape that styling had asked for, there's no way that

419
00:30:18,181 --> 00:30:22,780
we were going to be able to form essentially an S-bent shape.

420
00:30:22,781 --> 00:30:24,320
It was physically impossible.

421
00:30:24,321 --> 00:30:27,260
So we came up with the idea of a third volume.

422
00:30:27,261 --> 00:30:31,840
The two pipes coming out from the engine feed into a single chamber, the so-called third

423
00:30:31,841 --> 00:30:35,660
volume, creating a cleverly concealed extra space.

424
00:30:35,661 --> 00:30:44,640
This third volume served as the chamber where we flow two into one into two.

425
00:30:44,641 --> 00:30:49,380
One stumbling block that remained was getting the notorious radiator cover, nicknamed Gumby's

426
00:30:49,381 --> 00:30:53,200
Casket, to both look good and to work.

427
00:30:53,201 --> 00:30:57,000
Without good air circulation, the engine would overheat.

428
00:30:57,001 --> 00:31:00,980
In wind tunnel tests, they found they could get air flowing to the radiator, they just

429
00:31:00,981 --> 00:31:02,800
couldn't keep it.

430
00:31:02,801 --> 00:31:05,880
Once we had these big air scoops, we could get them to grab air, but the air would go

431
00:31:05,881 --> 00:31:08,040
in one side and out the other.

432
00:31:08,041 --> 00:31:13,260
A breakthrough finally came using an air scoop made out of a most unlikely material, a Federal

433
00:31:13,261 --> 00:31:14,260
Express box.

434
00:31:14,261 --> 00:31:18,980
One of the professors at the wind tunnel introduced us to the idea of putting some vortex generators

435
00:31:18,981 --> 00:31:20,920
into the backside of the cover.

436
00:31:20,921 --> 00:31:25,000
A vortex generator is a fancy name for a fin that stirs up the air.

437
00:31:25,660 --> 00:31:29,240
So we just took cardboard and taped these little veins.

438
00:31:29,640 --> 00:31:34,320
And when we did that, we created a turbulence in there that allowed more air to flow through the system

439
00:31:34,321 --> 00:31:36,320
and really improved the performance.

440
00:31:43,140 --> 00:31:46,880
At Porsche in Germany, the engine team was finally on a roll.

441
00:31:46,881 --> 00:31:52,480
A new prototype delivered in early 1998 was put into the latest mock-up of the bike.

442
00:31:53,060 --> 00:31:57,280
The engineers decided to put it to the ultimate challenge, Willie G's first ride.

443
00:31:58,920 --> 00:32:03,580
The true signature of whether or not this is a Harley Davidson is Willie Davidson.

444
00:32:10,340 --> 00:32:15,940
Willie's first ride, as I recall, he was one of only two or three vice presidents that day

445
00:32:15,941 --> 00:32:19,620
that were able to ride the bike because, quite frankly, Willie broke the bike.

446
00:32:24,240 --> 00:32:28,480
I laugh now. Maybe it wasn't so funny that day, but it was a prototype,

447
00:32:28,760 --> 00:32:30,860
and there were some parts in there that were handmade,

448
00:32:31,240 --> 00:32:38,500
and I managed to lock it in fourth gear and had to bring it in.

449
00:32:38,760 --> 00:32:42,640
But I got to ride it, and I had a big smile from ear to ear.

450
00:32:42,641 --> 00:32:49,780
Even though he had broken the thing, when he exclaimed that this is an expletive Harley-Davidson, we knew that we were on to something.

451
00:32:51,760 --> 00:32:55,440
The challenge now was making an engine that would hold up for the long run.

452
00:32:56,200 --> 00:33:01,740
As a benchmark to shoot for, the joint engineering team suggested the infamous Dusseldorf test.

453
00:33:02,700 --> 00:33:10,560
The test simulated a grueling trip from Porsche's facility in Weissach to Dusseldorf along Germany's notorious high-speed Autobahn.

454
00:33:12,640 --> 00:33:26,640
It would get all elements of city speeds, of climbing hills, of going through corners,

455
00:33:26,840 --> 00:33:34,760
good straight Audubon stretches of unlimited speed.

456
00:33:34,761 --> 00:33:41,040
The folklore is if you can survive that test, you can survive any test.

457
00:33:41,041 --> 00:33:44,500
Because it was impractical to monitor the test on the actual roads,

458
00:33:44,800 --> 00:33:47,860
it was run on an engine simulator called a dynamometer.

459
00:33:50,380 --> 00:33:55,940
To pass, the engines had to endure an obstacle course of wide-open throttle and mountain-climbing torque.

460
00:33:56,400 --> 00:34:00,000
The goal was 500 hours, nearly 21 days non-stop.

461
00:34:00,320 --> 00:34:05,180
It probably got to about 50 hours before we started seeing some issues.

462
00:34:05,181 --> 00:34:12,360
Because it wasn't a car engine, some suggested that a lower number might be more realistic.

463
00:34:14,160 --> 00:34:17,220
You could argue that maybe this was too aggressive.

464
00:34:17,660 --> 00:34:20,540
You could say, okay, 400 hours is fine.

465
00:34:20,980 --> 00:34:23,700
You know, we made it that far, but we did not.

466
00:34:23,820 --> 00:34:25,200
We said 500 hours.

467
00:34:25,620 --> 00:34:29,680
For well over a year, one engine after another was pushed to the breaking point.

468
00:34:30,340 --> 00:34:34,780
The joint engineering team learned from each setback, made improvements, then tried again.

469
00:34:35,180 --> 00:34:36,813
Their tenacity paid off.

470
00:34:36,814 --> 00:34:41,780
In March 2000, an engine passed the 400-hour point and was still running.

471
00:34:43,960 --> 00:34:48,640
People were calling on 20-minute intervals, you know, are we there yet, are we there yet?

472
00:34:48,720 --> 00:34:52,020
We're getting very close, we're getting very close. We all have our fingers crossed.

473
00:34:52,700 --> 00:34:57,960
The engine that got off to such a difficult start was now exceeding everyone's wildest expectations,

474
00:34:58,780 --> 00:35:03,560
cranking out 115 horsepower and revving at over 9,000 RPMs.

475
00:35:03,561 --> 00:35:09,800
With a top speed of over 140 miles per hour, it would be Harley's fastest, most powerful engine ever.

476
00:35:10,380 --> 00:35:13,540
It was so innovative, they decided to call it the revolution.

477
00:35:18,720 --> 00:35:22,620
But there was still one small hurdle to clear, parades.

478
00:35:22,940 --> 00:35:27,540
This is also a Harley-Davidson, and if it's a Harley, it's got to withstand parade duty.

479
00:35:27,541 --> 00:35:35,720
A problem that remained at that point was keeping the vehicle cool in idling conditions.

480
00:35:35,721 --> 00:35:40,660
The tightly packaged engine left little room for a large cooling fan, so engineers tried

481
00:35:40,661 --> 00:35:44,040
two strategically placed smaller fans.

482
00:35:44,041 --> 00:35:48,060
To put them to the test, they headed to the hottest spot they could find.

483
00:35:48,061 --> 00:35:54,760
At the Arizona Proving Ground, daytime temperatures can easily hit 120 degrees.

484
00:35:54,761 --> 00:35:59,440
To test the bike's ability to stay cool at idle, they're first taken on a 50-minute warm-up

485
00:35:59,441 --> 00:36:04,480
ride around the five-mile track.

486
00:36:04,481 --> 00:36:07,860
Once they're warmed up, they're rolled into something called a heat shed.

487
00:36:07,861 --> 00:36:12,200
The ambient temperature has to be a minimum of about 100 degrees.

488
00:36:12,201 --> 00:36:16,360
And then the vehicle is driven into a shed, which is nothing more than four walls and

489
00:36:16,361 --> 00:36:21,760
an open roof, and it's closed with the hot sun coming down on the vehicle.

490
00:36:21,761 --> 00:36:25,400
and the vehicle has to be able to idle for a period of time

491
00:36:25,401 --> 00:36:28,400
without either shutting down or overheating.

492
00:36:38,140 --> 00:36:40,300
When the time is up, the bikes are taken out

493
00:36:40,301 --> 00:36:42,820
and ridden for another hour in the blazing heat,

494
00:36:42,821 --> 00:36:44,240
then put back in the shed.

495
00:36:46,820 --> 00:36:48,640
This goes on all day.

496
00:36:48,641 --> 00:36:51,380
To everyone's relief, the bikes ran for eight hours

497
00:36:51,381 --> 00:36:54,880
without overheating once, the revolution engine had passed.

498
00:37:06,440 --> 00:37:09,160
After nearly four years of design and engineering,

499
00:37:09,161 --> 00:37:11,760
prototypes of Harley's top secret performance bike

500
00:37:11,761 --> 00:37:14,040
had to face their toughest challenge yet,

501
00:37:14,041 --> 00:37:16,500
months of real life durability tests.

502
00:37:16,501 --> 00:37:24,360
To guard against unauthorized publicity on the work in progress bike, the distinctive

503
00:37:24,361 --> 00:37:29,000
aluminum bodies were spray painted flat black.

504
00:37:29,001 --> 00:37:34,500
Even if someone did get a photo, it was going to be difficult for them to really make out

505
00:37:34,501 --> 00:37:43,540
the shapes.

506
00:37:43,541 --> 00:37:49,100
The bikes were ridden as much as 80,000 miles in a variety of conditions to test which parts

507
00:37:49,101 --> 00:37:53,000
held up and which part didn't.

508
00:37:53,001 --> 00:37:57,540
After each eight hour shift, the riders got to give their opinions about what they liked

509
00:37:57,541 --> 00:37:59,760
and what they didn't.

510
00:37:59,761 --> 00:38:06,100
We will test for engine noise, engine vibration, suspension, seating position, headlights,

511
00:38:06,101 --> 00:38:11,480
mirror positioning, brakes, acceleration, you name it.

512
00:38:11,481 --> 00:38:14,180
We run it through its paces.

513
00:38:14,181 --> 00:38:18,500
In addition to straight track, the bikes were also run several thousand miles on the

514
00:38:18,501 --> 00:38:19,960
grueling bump course.

515
00:38:19,961 --> 00:38:23,480
The point of us running that is to try to put as much damage into that vehicle in the

516
00:38:23,481 --> 00:38:25,440
shortest amount of time as possible.

517
00:38:25,441 --> 00:38:29,560
It shakes us, the bike, but we find failures out there that you wouldn't find anywhere

518
00:38:29,561 --> 00:38:30,560
else.

519
00:38:30,561 --> 00:38:34,940
To test for road conditions that are harder to find in real life, another group of prototype

520
00:38:34,941 --> 00:38:38,680
bikes were sent to Harley's test labs in Milwaukee.

521
00:38:38,681 --> 00:38:43,740
The reverb chamber is a copper-lined room used to bombard the bike with a barrage of

522
00:38:43,741 --> 00:38:44,740
radio waves.

523
00:38:44,741 --> 00:38:49,220
The test simulates the environment you might see outside.

524
00:38:49,221 --> 00:38:54,600
CB radios, AM and FM radios, cellular phones, anything that uses radio waves.

525
00:38:54,601 --> 00:38:58,960
The idea is to test for every kind of electronic interference.

526
00:38:58,961 --> 00:39:02,260
Antennas shoot out waves that bounce off the copper walls.

527
00:39:02,261 --> 00:39:06,440
A rotating fan stirs the radiation into a full-scale bombardment.

528
00:39:06,441 --> 00:39:09,000
Sort of like a big microwave oven.

529
00:39:09,001 --> 00:39:23,001
We often joke that we could pop more popcorn here than anyone else if we wanted to.

530
00:39:23,720 --> 00:39:26,220
Each bike then spent a day in the hogwash.

531
00:39:26,221 --> 00:39:31,680
For 24 hours straight, 30 high-pressured jets of water blasted the bike from every direction

532
00:39:31,681 --> 00:39:34,240
just to see if anything leaked or shorted out.

533
00:39:34,241 --> 00:39:40,000
What we're looking to find out is the water intrusion into the vehicle, in the speedometers,

534
00:39:40,001 --> 00:39:46,680
into tour packs, saddle bags, any place that we wouldn't want the water to be.

535
00:39:46,681 --> 00:39:59,240
After the rinse cycle, the first thing they checked was, would it run?

536
00:39:59,241 --> 00:40:03,740
Next up, bike parts were pounded, twisted and shaken in Harley's structures lab for

537
00:40:03,741 --> 00:40:04,740
weeks on end.

538
00:40:04,741 --> 00:40:10,620
The purpose of the structures lab is to make sure that the components that go on a motorcycle

539
00:40:10,621 --> 00:40:13,340
are not going to fail in service.

540
00:40:13,341 --> 00:40:18,980
You want to figure out what it takes to actually break it and then you also want to figure

541
00:40:18,981 --> 00:40:21,840
out how long it will live with a normal input.

542
00:40:21,841 --> 00:40:25,480
So you're looking for the week length.

543
00:40:25,481 --> 00:40:30,360
The full vehicle simulator puts the bike on a bone-jarring hell ride over a simulated road

544
00:40:30,361 --> 00:40:34,800
course that includes endless bumps, potholes, and torn up pavement.

545
00:40:34,801 --> 00:40:45,760
It goes on nonstop for five days.

546
00:40:45,761 --> 00:40:49,620
Parts were also tested for how well they held up against the elements.

547
00:40:49,621 --> 00:40:54,220
The entire concept of the vehicle is very much metallic.

548
00:40:54,221 --> 00:41:01,200
We had to figure out how to make it stay that way for as long as somebody owned a motorcycle.

549
00:41:01,201 --> 00:41:07,300
Test chambers subjected parts to endless pea soup fog and salt water air.

550
00:41:07,301 --> 00:41:11,600
Several lucky parts got to spend a few years catching rays up on the roof of one of Harley's

551
00:41:11,601 --> 00:41:20,740
plants to see how they handled blistering sun and acid rain.

552
00:41:20,741 --> 00:41:23,500
The real test of a Harley is what it sounds like.

553
00:41:28,380 --> 00:41:31,140
We go into a lot of trouble to protect

554
00:41:31,141 --> 00:41:33,440
the sound of the motorcycle because Harley

555
00:41:33,441 --> 00:41:34,840
has a legendary sound.

556
00:41:34,841 --> 00:41:37,440
People talk about the sound of our bikes.

557
00:41:40,460 --> 00:41:42,320
The bike's sound was fine-tuned

558
00:41:42,321 --> 00:41:44,300
in Harley's anechoic chamber.

559
00:41:45,860 --> 00:41:48,960
Anechoic stands for a room without echoes.

560
00:41:48,961 --> 00:41:54,340
In the room, we have a robotic system that takes an array of microphones around the bike.

561
00:41:54,341 --> 00:41:57,940
When we're trying to understand where a noise is coming from, a little whistle or a little

562
00:41:57,941 --> 00:42:04,420
ting, we can create this holographic field around the bike and zoom in right to where

563
00:42:04,421 --> 00:42:08,920
the noise is coming from.

564
00:42:08,921 --> 00:42:13,640
Right away, you can start zoning in on things that just don't sound harley.

565
00:42:13,641 --> 00:42:16,220
that talk a little more than they should,

566
00:42:16,660 --> 00:42:19,200
gears that may whine a little more than they should.

567
00:42:19,380 --> 00:42:21,000
Those are the first things we address.

568
00:42:24,440 --> 00:42:27,320
They also had to make sure the bike could pass new,

569
00:42:27,580 --> 00:42:30,240
increasingly tough noise regulations around the world.

570
00:42:31,180 --> 00:42:33,760
Throughout the planet, they have different regulations

571
00:42:33,761 --> 00:42:35,660
that call for an accelerated test.

572
00:42:35,661 --> 00:42:39,160
The track outside is a regulatory test track,

573
00:42:39,260 --> 00:42:41,180
and we find the loudest sound pressure level,

574
00:42:41,360 --> 00:42:42,540
and that's the number that's reported.

575
00:42:42,541 --> 00:42:47,540
It's really for making sure that the comfort of the people around you isn't disturbed.

576
00:42:47,541 --> 00:42:55,220
As the bike near completion, Willie G's son Bill Davidson and other members of Harley's

577
00:42:55,221 --> 00:43:00,000
marketing department struggled with one of the biggest challenges of all, what to call

578
00:43:00,001 --> 00:43:01,000
it.

579
00:43:01,001 --> 00:43:04,000
We had literally hundreds of names.

580
00:43:04,001 --> 00:43:06,000
This one, toaster.

581
00:43:06,001 --> 00:43:07,000
Toaster.

582
00:43:07,001 --> 00:43:08,000
I'm riding a toaster.

583
00:43:08,001 --> 00:43:09,000
I'm riding a toaster.

584
00:43:09,001 --> 00:43:18,022
toaster. I'm not sure that one works. It actually took us over a year.

585
00:43:18,023 --> 00:43:21,760
Full Belly VRC, I don't think

586
00:43:21,761 --> 00:43:22,961
we like that one. No.

587
00:43:22,962 --> 00:43:27,420
The minute you find one you like, you find out that it's trademarked in Japan.

588
00:43:27,720 --> 00:43:31,860
We had one name we liked and found out it was the name of a toilet bowl cleaner in Europe, so it's

589
00:43:31,861 --> 00:43:33,100
OK, that one can't work.

590
00:43:33,101 --> 00:43:40,700
The key element was having a name that when you saw this name,

591
00:43:40,701 --> 00:43:42,580
you could see this bike.

592
00:43:42,581 --> 00:43:44,720
So the two were locked together.

593
00:43:44,721 --> 00:43:46,240
And after coming up with a lot of names

594
00:43:46,241 --> 00:43:49,720
and whittling it down, the one that kind of kept coming up

595
00:43:49,721 --> 00:43:53,500
that people really started to gravitate toward was V-Rod.

596
00:43:53,501 --> 00:43:57,860
V for the V-twin engine, VR for the VR1000,

597
00:43:57,861 --> 00:44:01,040
rod for its dragster hot rod style.

598
00:44:01,041 --> 00:44:05,360
With everything finally in place, it was time to gear up the assembly line.

599
00:44:05,361 --> 00:44:10,360
Both the engine and the bike itself would be put together at Harley's newest plant in Kansas City.

600
00:44:16,360 --> 00:44:23,480
At the head of the new V-Rod assembly line would be the world's first fully automated pipe cutting robot.

601
00:44:23,481 --> 00:44:28,280
Using cameras for eyes, it could recognize whatever length pipe it was given,

602
00:44:28,281 --> 00:44:33,040
cut it with a precision laser, then do its own quality check of the end result.

603
00:44:34,120 --> 00:44:36,860
The successful partnership with Porsche continued.

604
00:44:37,560 --> 00:44:42,220
The revolution engine would be built as a joint venture in the same plant as the rest of the bike.

605
00:44:43,660 --> 00:44:47,280
Though the V-Rod wouldn't go into full production until after the dealer show,

606
00:44:47,281 --> 00:44:49,920
the assembly line had to be put through its paces.

607
00:44:51,520 --> 00:44:56,420
Setting up for mass production and adapting to an assembly line created new challenges for Harley.

608
00:44:56,421 --> 00:45:02,000
Because of the lean, low design of the bike, it proved difficult to fit the engine into the frame.

609
00:45:02,380 --> 00:45:03,117
The solution?

610
00:45:03,118 --> 00:45:07,140
Two removable rails that would also make it easier to service the bike.

611
00:45:12,400 --> 00:45:14,340
It began as a simple idea.

612
00:45:15,020 --> 00:45:20,620
Now, almost six years later, after hundreds of unexpected hurdles and hard-won breakthroughs,

613
00:45:20,621 --> 00:45:24,040
after thousands of hours of both disappointment and success,

614
00:45:24,041 --> 00:45:30,540
After millions of dollars invested in a melding of art and science, the V-Rod was finally a reality.

615
00:45:38,280 --> 00:45:43,440
The official launch of the V-Rod was scheduled for the annual Harley-Davidson dealer meeting,

616
00:45:43,800 --> 00:45:46,220
held this year at the Los Angeles Convention Center.

617
00:45:50,560 --> 00:45:53,420
For the team of engineers and designers, this was it.

618
00:45:54,040 --> 00:46:00,660
They had set out to build a bike that was unlike anything Harley had ever made, but that was also unmistakably a Harley.

619
00:46:00,980 --> 00:46:07,180
To respect a great American tradition, but also to grow and extend that tradition into the future.

620
00:46:11,780 --> 00:46:15,420
It's been an emotional journey that I will never forget.

621
00:46:15,421 --> 00:46:27,220
The fact that we have been able to execute a product that is this much different than everything else we've done is, again, a testimony to this organization.

622
00:46:27,940 --> 00:46:29,480
I will always be proud of that.

623
00:46:29,680 --> 00:46:37,000
Later this year, there's going to be a bike out there that I had something to do with every single part, and I don't know how I'm going to react.

624
00:46:37,320 --> 00:46:45,160
When you actually get on the bike and ride, it makes you so proud to know that this is something that we created from scratch.

625
00:46:45,420 --> 00:46:49,500
I mean, without us, this product would not be here.

626
00:46:50,660 --> 00:46:53,100
When you work together this long and this hard

627
00:46:53,101 --> 00:46:55,420
with this much passion, you never forget it.

628
00:46:55,421 --> 00:46:58,240
It's been five years now, roughly five years.

629
00:46:58,241 --> 00:47:00,340
So we're walking by it, we're looking at it,

630
00:47:00,341 --> 00:47:03,300
and we're taking it for granted to some degree.

631
00:47:03,301 --> 00:47:05,500
And to see other people's responses to it

632
00:47:05,501 --> 00:47:06,740
is gonna be phenomenal.

633
00:47:10,220 --> 00:47:12,180
When they take that cover off at the dealer's show,

634
00:47:12,181 --> 00:47:14,440
I guarantee you that I'm gonna have to

635
00:47:14,441 --> 00:47:16,200
turn away a little bit.

636
00:47:16,201 --> 00:47:17,360
Why is it?

637
00:47:17,361 --> 00:47:18,880
Well, you're a Harley guy.

638
00:47:18,881 --> 00:47:19,880
Nobody can see you cry.

639
00:47:29,400 --> 00:47:31,880
It's really fantastic to be standing here

640
00:47:31,881 --> 00:47:35,600
knowing that a new Harley classic is just about to be

641
00:47:35,601 --> 00:47:37,680
sprung on the world, and we did it.

642
00:47:37,681 --> 00:47:41,040
I spent half my professional career at Harley working on this.

643
00:47:41,041 --> 00:47:41,840
And here it is.

644
00:47:41,841 --> 00:47:43,040
We're finally going to get to show it.

645
00:47:43,041 --> 00:47:45,140
We're finally gonna get to talk about it.

646
00:47:45,141 --> 00:47:46,320
It's almost hard to describe.

647
00:47:46,321 --> 00:47:47,680
We're gonna shock the world.

648
00:47:47,681 --> 00:47:48,520
This is it.

649
00:47:48,521 --> 00:47:49,660
It's gonna be awesome.

650
00:47:50,980 --> 00:47:55,420
Ladies and gentlemen, let's give a rousing welcome

651
00:47:55,421 --> 00:47:58,020
to Willie G and Bill Davidson

652
00:47:58,021 --> 00:48:00,600
and the newest member of our family,

653
00:48:00,601 --> 00:48:03,360
the Harley Davidson, the Rod!

654
00:48:43,040 --> 00:48:46,540
What do they think?

655
00:48:46,541 --> 00:48:47,540
I think it's awesome.

656
00:48:47,541 --> 00:48:48,540
It's definitely a Harley.

657
00:48:48,541 --> 00:48:50,540
This is going to rock.

658
00:48:50,541 --> 00:48:52,540
Rock the motorcycle world.

659
00:48:52,541 --> 00:48:54,540
Man, I just can't believe it.

660
00:48:54,541 --> 00:48:55,540
It's true.

661
00:48:55,541 --> 00:48:56,540
It's breathtaking.

662
00:48:56,541 --> 00:48:58,540
B-Rock!

